abstract: "We study a two-digit coordinate model of maps on the Galois ring GR(4,m), with q = 2^m and N = q^2 = |GR(4,m)|, in which the ring addition carries from the low digit into the high digit. For maps F(a,b) = (p(a), h(a) ⊕ d(a)b) with p an APN permutation, d a nowhere-vanishing F_2-affine map whose linear part has a one-dimensional kernel, and h arbitrary, we prove direction-wise differential counts for B = J ∘ F, a key-averaged two-block bound of order 1/N, and a collision-energy bound, all uniform in h. In contrast, a local integral property of the same two-block map depends on h; for the inverse map and every admissible d we give a linear certificate on h that excludes a family of local zero sums, and an explicit correction of Boolean degree two of every h that fails it; degree two is minimal for this local problem. For the averaged alternating two-word arrangement we prove a one-word contraction of order q^{-(1-(1/2)log_2 3)}, uniform in h and in the admissible data, and deduce that a fixed number of steps brings the averaged two-word difference distribution to the scale 1/N. For the inverse map with d(a) = 1 + (a/ρ)^4 + (a/ρ)^2, where m is odd and ρ ≠ 0, the contraction improves to the order q^{-1/2}, which is optimal since no admissible data give a faster order; for other d our constant grows with the linearized degree of d. We then record what these statements do and do not imply for an independent-key composition: the averaged multi-word bound coexists with a candidate-existence distinguisher with data linear in the word size. No security claim for a concrete cipher is made."
